<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raichur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raichur is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with a fort begun in the late 13th century. It gives its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raichur-doab">Raichur Doab</a>, the fertile land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Tungabhadra rivers, which was fought over for centuries, first by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> and the Bahmani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> sultanates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> of Vijayanagara won a major victory at Raichur in 1520.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raichur.jpg" alt="Raichur" width="320" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tanzeelahad · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lake_Aam_Talab,_Raichur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Raichur is a city and the headquarters of Raichur district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Tungabhadra rivers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a> at an average elevation of 407 metres. Summers are hot, often well above 40 degrees, and rain falls mainly between May and August. Raichur Junction is an important station on the Mumbai-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> railway line.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The recorded history of the district goes back to the third century BCE. Three minor rock edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> are found there, one at Maski and two near Koppal, which shows the area was part of the Mauryan emperor's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, governed by a viceroy. It then seems to have belonged to the Satavahanas, later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakataka-dynasty">Vakatakas</a> and the Kadamba dominions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulakeshin-ii">Pulakeshin II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a> made it a province under his son Adityavarma, and from the eighth century it lay within the dominions of the Rashtrakutas.</p><p>Raichur Fort has inscriptions in Persian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> and Arabic that refer to its construction in the late 13th century, under the Kakatiya king Rudra. The fort passed to the Vijayanagara kingdom after the decline of the Kakatiyas, and was then disputed for nearly two centuries. The Bahmanis captured it in 1323. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saluva-narasimha">Saluva Narasimha Deva Raya</a> expressed in his testament a wish that Raichur be recaptured, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> had it in mind from his coronation in 1509.</p><p>In 1520 a dispute arose when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> sent a Muslim in his service, Saeed Maraikar, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> with money to buy horses and Maraikar instead went to Adil Khan and offered his services. Adil Khan refused to return him, and during the ensuing peace Krishnadevaraya made extensive preparations for a grand attack on the Raichur doab. Later the area belonged to the Hyderabad State, until the reorganisation of states on 1 November 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Raichur is rich in inscriptions, with hundreds ranging from the Mauryan period to the end of the Muslim period, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>, Telugu, Arabic and Persian. The most important sites are Maski, Koppal, Kuknur, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> gold mines, Mudgal, Lingsugur and Raichur itself. The ruins of the fort include many irrigation tanks and old temples. The city has a university of agricultural sciences and a university of its own.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raichur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great fortresses anchored Bahmani power, among them Gulbarga, Bidar, Parenda, Golconda and <b>Raichur</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early decades were spent fighting the neighbouring Deccan sultanates of Ahmadnagar, Golconda and Bidar, and the Vijayanagara Empire to the south over the fertile <b>Raichur</b> doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Abdur Razzaq described the capital as surrounded by several concentric lines of fortification, and a chain of strongholds, including <b>Raichur</b>, Gooty, Penukonda and Chandragiri, defended the frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>War with the Chola Empire for the fertile <b>Raichur</b> doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra and for influence over Vengi in coastal Andhra dominated the empire’s history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
